codemap 8
path archlint
kind layer
author ai
group tooling
note archlint: the repo's own linter, for every rule the compiler and clippy cannot hold. Each rule is one Rule variant with a code, one sentence stating the one way the code is written, and a check function over a tree-sitter-rust syntax tree. A finding prints as file:line: code: <the one way> (<what matched>), so the message is the rule: an agent learns it from the error at the moment it applies, and the only way to make the finding go away is to write the code that way. Nothing needs name resolution: a rule that would (only X may call Y) is turned into a crate or privacy boundary instead, so the compiler resolves it. Every file has a zone (strict, or test for tests/ and tests.rs) and arch::applies decides which rules run in which zone; a few rules further narrow themselves to a crate or file (the domain for I/O, the GUI's widget, theme, id and key modules, the scenario files for test registration), and the GUI rules exempt their own home module, which is where the one way lives. Suppressions are the one escape hatch and are themselves a rule: #[expect(lint, reason)] listed by file in arch.rs EXPECTS. The fixtures in lint/tests.rs pin each rule both ways: code that must fail and the closest code that must pass. The gate runs archlint over the workspace; the post-tool hook runs it on each edited file and hands the findings straight back.

